[38;2;124;124;124mFor any hyperbolic surface $S$ that admits pseudo-Anosov elements (which is any [0m[38;2;124;124;124msurface with $\chi(S) < 0$ except possibly some small cases), the mapping class [0m[38;2;124;124;124mgroup is not virtually cyclic, and the centralizer of any pseudo-Anosov element [0m[38;2;124;124;124mis virtually cyclic (McCarthy's theorem). So there always exist elements outside[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe centralizer.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me be even more careful. The problem says "hyperbolic surface [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$S$". A hyperbolic surface is one that admits a complete hyperbolic metric, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhich means $\chi(S) < 0$. For such surfaces, pseudo-Anosov elements exist (as [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mlong as the surface is not one of the trivial cases).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe surfaces with $\chi(S) < 0$ that have pseudo-Anosov elements: all of them [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexcept $S_{0,3}$ (which has $\chi = 1 > 0$ anyway, so not hyperbolic). Actually,[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$S_{0,3}$ has $\chi = 1$, not hyperbolic. $S_{0,4}$ has $\chi = -2 < 0$... wait,[0m[38;2;124;124;124msphere with 4 punctures: $\chi = 2 - 4 = -2 < 0$. But $Mod(S_{0,4}) \cong [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbb{Z}/2 \times \mathbb{Z}/2$? No, that's not right.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$Mod(S_{0,4}) \cong PSL(2,\mathbb{Z}) \cong \mathbb{Z}/2 * \mathbb{Z}/3$, which [0m[38;2;124;124;124mis virtually free and contains pseudo-Anosov elements.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mOK so for any hyperbolic surface $S$ (meaning $\chi(S) < 0$), the mapping class [0m[38;2;124;124;124mgroup contains pseudo-Anosov elements, and by McCarthy's theorem, the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcentralizer of any pseudo-Anosov element is virtually cyclic. Since $Mod(S)$ is [0m[38;2;124;124;124mnot virtually cyclic for any such surface (it contains a free group of rank $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\geq 2$), there exist elements outside the centralizer.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the proof is clean. Let me also address whether the question might be asking [0m[38;2;124;124;124mabout non-conjugate elements, since that's the more interesting version.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor non-conjugate elements with the same stretch factor: This is also true. One [0m[38;2;124;124;124mway to see this:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mConsider $f$ and $f^{-1}$. Both are pseudo-Anosov with $\lambda_f = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\lambda_{f^{-1}}$. If $f$ is not conjugate to $f^{-1}$ in $Mod(S)$, then we have[0m[38;2;124;124;124mnon-conjugate elements with the same stretch factor. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNot all pseudo-Anosov elements are reversible (conjugate to their inverse). In [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfact, "most" are not. For instance, on a generic surface, a generic pseudo-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mAnosov is not reversible.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut actually, even more is true: there exist pseudo-Anosov elements on the same [0m[38;2;124;124;124msurface with the same stretch factor that are not even related by any [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mautomorphism of the mapping class group. This follows from the fact that the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnumber of conjugacy classes of pseudo-Anosov elements with stretch factor $\leq [0m[38;2;124;124;124mL$ grows faster than the number of distinct stretch factors $\leq L$ (in some [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mappropriate sense).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut for the question as stated, the conjugation argument is sufficient and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mclean.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me also think about whether there's an even simpler argument that doesn't [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mrequire McCarthy's theorem.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSimple argument: Take any pseudo-Anosov $f$ on $S$. Since $Mod(S)$ is non-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtrivial (in fact, infinite and non-abelian for any hyperbolic surface), there [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexists $h \in Mod(S)$ that does not commute with $f$. Then $g = hfh^{-1} \neq f$[0m[38;2;124;124;124mand $\lambda_g = \lambda_f$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWhy does such $h$ exist? Because if every element of $Mod(S)$ commuted with $f$,[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthen $f$ would be in the center of $Mod(S)$. But the center of $Mod(S)$ is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtrivial (for closed surfaces of genus $\geq 3$, and more generally for surfaces [0m[38;2;124;124;124mwith $3g-3+n > 0$ except genus 2 where the hyperelliptic involution is central [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mbut finite order). A pseudo-Anosov element has infinite order, so it cannot be [0m
[38;2;124;124;124min the center (since the center is either trivial or generated by a finite-order[0m[38;2;124;124;124melement).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a cleaner argument. Let me use this.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me be precise about the center. For $Mod(S)$:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- If $S$ is a closed surface of genus $g \geq 3$: center is trivial.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- If $S$ is a closed surface of genus 2: center is $\mathbb{Z}/2$ generated by [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe hyperelliptic involution.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- If $S$ has boundary or punctures: center is trivial (for $3g-3+n > 0$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn all cases, the center contains only finite-order elements (or is trivial). A [0m[38;2;124;124;124mpseudo-Anosov element has infinite order, so it is not in the center. Therefore,[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthere exists $h$ that doesn't commute with $f$, and $hfh^{-1} \neq f$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mGreat, this is a clean proof. Let me write it up.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, I want to make sure about one thing: is the stretch factor indeed a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconjugacy invariant? Yes, this is standard. If $g = hfh^{-1}$, then $g$ acts on [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe foliations of $f$ (transported by $h$), and the stretch factor is the same. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mMore formally, the stretch factor is the topological entropy of the mapping [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mclass, which is a conjugacy invariant. Or: the stretch factor is the spectral [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mradius of the action on Teichmüller space, which is conjugation-invariant.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write the final proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, I realize I should also consider: the problem says "hyperbolic surface[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$S$". This could mean a specific fixed surface, and the question is whether on [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthat surface, there exist distinct pA elements with the same stretch factor. The[0m[38;2;124;124;124manswer is yes for any such surface (that admits pA elements).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write the proof now.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mProof:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Yes**, such elements exist.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet $S$ be a hyperbolic surface (i.e., $\chi(S) < 0$) that admits pseudo-Anosov [0m[38;2;124;124;124mmapping classes. Let $f \in Mod(S)$ be any pseudo-Anosov element with stretch [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfactor $\lambda_f > 1$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Claim**: There exists $g \in Mod(S)$, $g \neq f$, such that $g$ is pseudo-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mAnosov and $\lambda_g = \lambda_f$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 1: Stretch factor is a conjugacy invariant.**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf $h \in Mod(S)$ and $g = hfh^{-1}$, then $g$ is pseudo-Anosov (conjugation [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpreserves Nielsen-Thurston type) and $\lambda_g = \lambda_f$. This follows [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mbecause the stretch factor equals the exponential of the topological entropy, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhich is a conjugacy invariant. Equivalently, $g$ acts on the image of the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstable/unstable foliations of $f$ under $h$, with the same expansion/contraction[0m[38;2;124;124;124mfactors.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 2: $f$ is not central in $Mod(S)$.**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe center of $Mod(S)$ is well-understood:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- For surfaces with $3g - 3 + n > 0$ (where $g$ is the genus and $n$ the number [0m[38;2;124;124;124mof punctures/boundary components), excluding the closed genus-2 case, the center[0m[38;2;124;124;124mis trivial.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- For the closed genus-2 surface, the center is $\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgenerated by the hyperelliptic involution.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn all cases, every element of the center has finite order. Since a pseudo-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mAnosov mapping class has infinite order, $f$ is not in the center of $Mod(S)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 3: Construct $g$.**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince $f$ is not central, there exists $h \in Mod(S)$ such that $hf \neq fh$, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mi.e., $hfh^{-1} \neq f$. Set $g = hfh^{-1}$. Then:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $g \neq f$ (by choice of $h$),[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $g$ is pseudo-Anosov (conjugation preserves Nielsen-Thurston type),[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $\lambda_g = \lambda_f$ (by Step 1).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore, $f$ and $g$ are distinct pseudo-Anosov elements of $Mod(S)$ with [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mequal stretch factors. $\blacksquare$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is clean and correct. Let me also note that this is the "trivial" version [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mof the question (conjugate elements). The deeper question of non-conjugate [0m
[38;2;124;124;124melements with the same stretch factor also has a positive answer, but the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mquestion as stated only asks for distinct elements.[0m

